/-
Module: GaussianRegression.lean
Researcher/requester: Anders Hellström.

Purpose: Establish a genuine measure-theoretic Gaussian conditional-expectation
identity, using maintained mathlib rather than axiomatizing normal independence
or constructing a new conditional expectation integral.

Mathematical model: The observed vector Z takes values in a real Banach
space E, the innovation R is a real random variable, and (R,Z) has a joint
Gaussian law. If the covariance of every real linear functional of R with
every real continuous linear functional of Z is zero, joint Gaussianity
implies independence of the innovation and observed sigma algebra.

The future response is p(Z) + R where p(Z) is integrable and measurable
with respect to the sigma algebra generated by Z. When the centered
Gaussian innovation has mean zero, its conditional expectation is zero
and E[p(Z)+R | sigma(Z)] = p(Z) almost everywhere.

All hypotheses are explicit, including the actual joint Gaussian law,
measurability, covariance orthogonality, and the zero residual mean.
Crucially, independent noise is a *derived theorem*, not a hypothesis,
and the desired conditional expectation is never introduced as an axiom.

Library use: mathlib HasGaussianLaw.indepFun_of_covariance_strongDual,
IndepFun_iff_Indep, condExp_indep_eq, condExp_add, and
condExp_of_stronglyMeasurable. There are no admits, sorries or axioms.

Boundary: This is the complete Gaussian-to-CE probability bridge
parameterized by an orthogonality certificate and an adapted integrable
predictor. The deterministic equicorrelation inverse/covariance identities
already kernel-checked in Rocq supply the orthogonality calculations;
a cross-prover theorem connecting those Rocq formulas to this Lean
theorem is not yet claimed.

Reproduction: .github/workflows/gaussian-lean.yml using pinned mathlib.

AI disclosure: OpenAI assisted source generation and review. Only
the Lean kernel and explicit dependency library establish formal proof.
-/
import Mathlib.Probability.ConditionalExpectation
import Mathlib.Probability.Distributions.Gaussian.HasGaussianLaw.Independence
import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Function.ConditionalExpectation.Basic
import Mathlib.Tactic

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace CompositeIQ.GaussianRegression

variable {Ω E : Type*}
variable [MeasurableSpace Ω]
variable [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
variable [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
variable [SecondCountableTopology E] [CompleteSpace E]
variable {μ : Measure Ω} [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
variable {observed : Ω → E} {innovation predictor : Ω → ℝ}

/--
A jointly Gaussian innovation and observed vector, with zero cross-covariance
for every continuous linear functional, have independent generated sigma
algebras. This is the exact missing implication in a purely algebraic
least-squares derivation.

The theorem invokes mathlib's proved Gaussian independence theorem; no
independence is never postulated.
-/
theorem gaussian_innovation_independent_of_observations
    (hGaussian : HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (innovation ω, observed ω)) μ)
    (hCross : ∀ (L₁ : StrongDual ℝ ℝ) (L₂ : StrongDual ℝ E),
      covariance (L₁ ∘ innovation) (L₂ ∘ observed) μ = 0) :
    Indep (MeasurableSpace.comap innovation inferInstance)
      (MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance) μ := by
  exact (IndepFun_iff_Indep innovation observed μ).mp
    (hGaussian.indepFun_of_covariance_strongDual hCross)

/--
**Measure-theoretic Gaussian conditional expectation.** If a future
quantity is an observed-history-measurable, integrable predictor plus
a centered jointly Gaussian innovation orthogonal to all observed
linear functionals, the conditional expectation of the future quantity
given the full observed sigma algebra equals the predictor almost surely.

The proof derives Gaussian independence from covariance orthogonality,
then uses mathlib's conditional expectation for independent random
variables and conditional-expectation linearity. No assumed conditional
expectation is present in the hypotheses.
-/
theorem gaussian_regression_condExp
    (hGaussian : HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (innovation ω, observed ω)) μ)
    (hCross : ∀ (L₁ : StrongDual ℝ ℝ) (L₂ : StrongDual ℝ E),
      covariance (L₁ ∘ innovation) (L₂ ∘ observed) μ = 0)
    (hObs : Measurable observed)
    (hInnovation : Measurable innovation)
    (hPredictor :
      StronglyMeasurable[MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] predictor)
    (hPredictorInt : Integrable predictor μ)
    (hInnovationMean : (∫ ω, innovation ω ∂μ) = 0) :
    μ[predictor + innovation | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance]
      =ᵐ[μ] predictor := by
  -- No local measurable-space instances are introduced: the ambient
  -- probability space must remain the same throughout the independence
  -- and conditional expectation statements.
  have hIndep :
      Indep (MeasurableSpace.comap innovation inferInstance)
        (MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance) μ :=
    gaussian_innovation_independent_of_observations hGaussian hCross
  have hStrongInnovation :
      StronglyMeasurable[MeasurableSpace.comap innovation inferInstance]
        innovation := by
    have hm :
        Measurable[MeasurableSpace.comap innovation inferInstance]
          innovation := by
      exact measurable_iff_comap_le.mpr le_rfl
    exact hm.stronglyMeasurable
  have hInnovCE :
      μ[innovation | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
        (fun _ => (0 : ℝ)) := by
    have hIndCE :=
      condExp_indep_eq hInnovation.comap_le hObs.comap_le
        hStrongInnovation hIndep
    simpa only [hInnovationMean] using hIndCE
  have hPredCE :
      μ[predictor | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] = predictor :=
    condExp_of_stronglyMeasurable hObs.comap_le hPredictor hPredictorInt
  have hInnovationInt : Integrable innovation μ := hGaussian.fst.integrable
  refine (condExp_add hPredictorInt hInnovationInt
    (MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance)).trans ?_
  filter_upwards [hInnovCE] with ω hzero
  simp [hPredCE, hzero]

/--
The equicorrelation regression coefficient is defined by the canonical
common-mode eigenvalue d_n(c) = 1 + (n-1)c. We use Mathlib's finite-sum
library directly for the actual observed score vector. This definition
is algebraic; the exact covariance domain and nonzero d_n(c) are
assumptions of its probability-theoretic theorem.
-/
noncomputable def equicorrelationPredictor (n : ℕ) (c : ℝ)
    (observed : Fin n → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  c / (1 + ((n : ℝ) - 1) * c) * ∑ i, observed i

/--
The future-score regression innovation is the response minus its
equicorrelated observed-score linear projection. This is an explicit
pointwise definition; Gaussian independence is NOT assumed by it.
-/
noncomputable def equicorrelationInnovation {n : ℕ} (c : ℝ)
    (future : Ω → ℝ) (observed : Ω → Fin n → ℝ) : Ω → ℝ :=
  fun ω => future ω - equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)

/--
**Exact formula, measure-theoretic conditional expectation.**

For an observed vector of n standardized tests and a future response,
the conditional expectation on the entire observed-history sigma
algebra equals c*S_n/d_n(c) almost surely. The assumptions identify
the regression residual as jointly Gaussian with the full observed
vector and certify zero covariance of the residual against every
continuous linear observable of the history. Mathlib's Gaussian
independence theorem discharges the independence requirement; the
conditional-expectation universal property is discharged by Mathlib's
conditional expectation lemmas rather than assumed as a premise.

The equicorrelation covariance-domain checks proving the zero
covariance certificate are separately kernel-verified in the project's
Rocq library; this theorem does not yet re-prove those cross-covariance
calculations in Lean or claim a cross-prover certificate. In particular,
joint Gaussianity of the residual/observed pair is an explicit model
hypothesis; it may be obtained from joint Gaussianity of the original
future/observed vector by the standard linear transformation closure.

This proof is valid for admissible negative c as well as positive c,
provided the full Gaussian law and the listed side conditions hold.
-/
theorem gaussian_equicorrelated_history_condExp
    {n : ℕ} (c : ℝ) (future : Ω → ℝ)
    (observed : Ω → Fin n → ℝ)
    (hD : 1 + ((n : ℝ) - 1) * c ≠ 0)
    (hGaussian : HasGaussianLaw
      (fun ω => (equicorrelationInnovation c future observed ω, observed ω)) μ)
    (hCross : ∀ (L₁ : StrongDual ℝ ℝ)
        (L₂ : StrongDual ℝ (Fin n → ℝ)),
      covariance (L₁ ∘ equicorrelationInnovation c future observed)
        (L₂ ∘ observed) μ = 0)
    (hObs : Measurable observed)
    (hInnovation : Measurable (equicorrelationInnovation c future observed))
    (hPredictor : StronglyMeasurable[
        MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance]
      (fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)))
    (hPredictorInt : Integrable
      (fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)) μ)
    (hInnovationMean :
      (∫ ω, equicorrelationInnovation c future observed ω ∂μ) = 0) :
    μ[future | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
      (fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)) := by
  have hDecomposition :
      (fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)) +
        equicorrelationInnovation c future observed = future := by
    funext ω
    simp only [Pi.add_apply, equicorrelationInnovation]
    ring
  have hCE :=
    gaussian_regression_condExp
      (observed := observed)
      (innovation := equicorrelationInnovation c future observed)
      (predictor := fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω))
      hGaussian hCross hObs hInnovation hPredictor
      hPredictorInt hInnovationMean
  rw [hDecomposition] at hCE
  exact hCE

/--
**Gaussian affine-regression closure under linear transformations.**

Starting only from a jointly Gaussian pair (future score, observed-score
vector), a continuous linear projection L of the observed vector
produces a jointly Gaussian pair (future score minus L(observed),
observed). This is the exact closure property needed to avoid assuming
separately that the regression residual is jointly Gaussian.

The construction reuses the standard continuous-linear projections from
a product, their composition, subtraction and pair construction, and
mathlib's HasGaussianLaw.map_fun. It does not reimplement Gaussian
characteristic functions or define a new probability measure.
-/
theorem gaussian_joint_linear_residual
    {future : Ω → ℝ} {observed : Ω → E}
    (L : E →L[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hJoint : HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (future ω, observed ω)) μ) :
    HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (future ω - L (observed ω), observed ω)) μ := by
  let first : (ℝ × E) →L[ℝ] ℝ :=
    ContinuousLinearMap.fst ℝ ℝ E
  let second : (ℝ × E) →L[ℝ] E :=
    ContinuousLinearMap.snd ℝ ℝ E
  let regressionTransform : (ℝ × E) →L[ℝ] (ℝ × E) :=
    (first - L.comp second).prod second
  have hTransformed := hJoint.map_fun regressionTransform
  simpa [regressionTransform, first, second] using hTransformed

/--
**Measure-theoretic Gaussian linear-regression conditional expectation
with the joint normality hypothesis on the ORIGINAL observations.**

Suppose (Y,Z) is jointly Gaussian, L is a continuous linear predictor,
Y and Z are measurable, the residual Y-L(Z) is centered, and its
covariance with every continuous linear functional of Z is zero.
By the previously kernel-checked linear closure theorem, (Y-L(Z),Z)
is jointly Gaussian. Mathlib then proves the residual independent of
the entire observed sigma algebra, and the conditional-expectation
universal property identifies E[Y | sigma(Z)] = L(Z) a.e.

In contrast to the first Gaussian CE theorem, this statement DOES NOT
assume the transformed residual has a Gaussian joint law: it derives
that fact from original joint Gaussianity using mathlib's maintained
closure theorem. The remaining equicorrelation-specific task is
discharging the explicit covariance orthogonality premise from the
known full-battery covariance matrix, already established algebraically
in Rocq but not yet mechanically bridged across provers.
-/
theorem gaussian_linear_projection_condExp
    {future : Ω → ℝ} {observed : Ω → E}
    (L : E →L[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hJoint : HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (future ω, observed ω)) μ)
    (hCovarianceOrthogonal :
      ∀ (A : StrongDual ℝ ℝ) (B : StrongDual ℝ E),
        covariance
          (A ∘ (fun ω => future ω - L (observed ω)))
          (B ∘ observed) μ = 0)
    (hObservedMeasurable : Measurable observed)
    (hFutureMeasurable : Measurable future)
    (hCenteredResidual :
      (∫ ω, future ω - L (observed ω) ∂μ) = 0) :
    μ[future | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
      (fun ω => L (observed ω)) := by
  have hGaussianResidual :
      HasGaussianLaw
        (fun ω => (future ω - L (observed ω), observed ω)) μ :=
    gaussian_joint_linear_residual L hJoint
  have hResidualMeasurable :
      Measurable (fun ω => future ω - L (observed ω)) :=
    hFutureMeasurable.sub (L.continuous.measurable.comp hObservedMeasurable)
  have hObsComap :
      Measurable[MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] observed :=
    measurable_iff_comap_le.mpr le_rfl
  have hPredictorStrong :
      StronglyMeasurable[MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance]
        (fun ω => L (observed ω)) :=
    (L.continuous.measurable.comp hObsComap).stronglyMeasurable
  have hPredictorIntegrable :
      Integrable (fun ω => L (observed ω)) μ :=
    (hJoint.snd.map_fun L).integrable
  have hCE :=
    gaussian_regression_condExp
      (observed := observed)
      (innovation := fun ω => future ω - L (observed ω))
      (predictor := fun ω => L (observed ω))
      hGaussianResidual hCovarianceOrthogonal
      hObservedMeasurable hResidualMeasurable
      hPredictorStrong hPredictorIntegrable hCenteredResidual
  have hPointwise :
      (fun ω => L (observed ω)) +
        (fun ω => future ω - L (observed ω)) = future := by
    funext ω
    simp only [Pi.add_apply]
    ring
  rw [hPointwise] at hCE
  exact hCE

/--
Continuous linear form implementing the exact equicorrelated predictor
from a finite observed vector. Reuse Mathlib's coordinate projections
and finite sums; no custom finite sum, matrix inverse or normed-space
construction is introduced.
-/
noncomputable def equicorrelationPredictorCLM (n : ℕ) (c : ℝ) :
    (Fin n → ℝ) →L[ℝ] ℝ :=
  (c / (1 + ((n : ℝ) - 1) * c)) •
    (∑ i : Fin n,
      (ContinuousLinearMap.proj i : (Fin n → ℝ) →L[ℝ] ℝ))

/--
The continuous linear form above evaluates to the previously checked
finite-score predictor c*S_n/d_n(c), retaining exactly the same scalar
common-mode denominator and using only library-backed finite sums.
-/
theorem equicorrelationPredictorCLM_apply (n : ℕ) (c : ℝ)
    (scores : Fin n → ℝ) :
    equicorrelationPredictorCLM n c scores =
      equicorrelationPredictor n c scores := by
  simp [equicorrelationPredictorCLM, equicorrelationPredictor,
    Finset.sum_apply, smul_eq_mul]

/--
**Joint-Gaussian equicorrelation conditional expectation for an
arbitrary finite observed-score vector.**

The model now assumes joint Gaussianity only for the original future
score and the observed vector, not for the innovation. The precise
equicorrelation prediction formula is realized as a continuous linear
map built out of the canonical coordinate projections and finite sums.

The residual zero-cross-covariance certificate remains explicit. In the
project's Rocq library the exchangeable inverse/Schur algebra proving
that certificate is kernel checked, but its correspondence to this
Mathlib covariance statement is a separate cross-prover obligation.
Given the certificate, Mathlib proves that the original Gaussian law
implies residual joint normality, residual independence, and the
measure-theoretic conditional-expectation equality a.s. It is valid
for negative as well as positive covariance parameters whenever the
full joint Gaussian model and nonzero determinant domain hold.
-/
theorem gaussian_equicorrelated_original_joint_condExp
    {n : ℕ} (c : ℝ) (future : Ω → ℝ)
    (observed : Ω → Fin n → ℝ)
    (hD : 1 + ((n : ℝ) - 1) * c ≠ 0)
    (hJoint :
      HasGaussianLaw (fun ω => (future ω, observed ω)) μ)
    (hCovarianceOrthogonal :
      ∀ (A : StrongDual ℝ ℝ) (B : StrongDual ℝ (Fin n → ℝ)),
        covariance
          (A ∘ (fun ω =>
            future ω - equicorrelationPredictorCLM n c (observed ω)))
          (B ∘ observed) μ = 0)
    (hObservedMeasurable : Measurable observed)
    (hFutureMeasurable : Measurable future)
    (hCenteredResidual :
      (∫ ω, future ω - equicorrelationPredictorCLM n c (observed ω) ∂μ) = 0) :
    μ[future | MeasurableSpace.comap observed inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
      (fun ω => equicorrelationPredictor n c (observed ω)) := by
  have hCE :=
    gaussian_linear_projection_condExp
      (L := equicorrelationPredictorCLM n c)
      hJoint hCovarianceOrthogonal hObservedMeasurable
      hFutureMeasurable hCenteredResidual
  simpa only [equicorrelationPredictorCLM_apply] using hCE

end CompositeIQ.GaussianRegression
